  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Producing fancy yarns in Bolivia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">On account of its vast herds of Llama and Alpaca kept at high altitude, Bolivia is well-known as producer and exporter of tops, yarns and garments made from the natural fibres of these animals. Its important knitwear sector is however lagging behind, due to its concentration on traditional winter garments produced from relatively heavy, plain yarns available in Bolivia.The idea behind the project is to boost the competitiveness of local knitwear companies and associated hand knitters, through the utilisation of so-called fancy yarns. Fancy yarns are made with distinctive colour changes, special texture, irregular profile and/or a blend of various fibres that make them stand out against traditional plain yarns. Still using mostly Alpaca and Llama fibre as a basis, fancy yarns allow the production of much lighter and diverse knitwear, also for summer collections and according to the latest trends in fashion.The applicant, Pitata S.A.C. from Arequipa, is active in the procurement of Alpaca fibre and in the commercialisation of yarns and tops for the textile industry in Peru and abroad. Local partner, Altifibers S.A. from El Alto, is a producer of fibre tops and yarns of Llama, Alpaca and sheep. Third partner, AslanTrends USA Corp from Washington, USA, is a producer of a wide variety of top quality yarn products from natural fibres, sourced from South America. The three partners will set up a joint venture to produce fancy yarns in Bolivia.The factory will be set up on the premises of the local partner in El Alto, and will create direct employment for 35 persons at the plant, as well contract another 40 independent makers of &quot;hanks&quot;, a very labour intensive activity. The staff will be extensively trained to acquire the know-how and skills required to produce high quality fancy yarns, which will be sold to the local industry as well as on the international market. A state-of-the-art air control system and waste water treatment plant, along with OHSAS 18001 certification, will ensure that the environment is protected and working conditions are healthy and safe.The proposed production of fancy yarns will generate considerable additional demand for Llama and Alpaca fibre, benefiting a large number of rural producers. The project will include a pilot to certify the natural fibre supply chain according to the Global Organic Textile Standard (GOTS), which will bring additional income to farmers through the organic premium.</narrative>
  </description>
